    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build is green. There's a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`), and both its tests pass, along with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS` for the full `mvn test`.\n\n## Summary\n\nConverted owner deletion to a soft-delete. Changes:\n\n**Domain / persistence**\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean column (defaulting to `false`, so a newly created owner is not deleted) with getter/setter.\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql` \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` column to the `owners` table (hsqldb is what the tests use; the others kept in sync).\n\n**Endpoint behavior**\n- `function/owner/DeleteOwner.java` \u2014 instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, it now `owner.setDeleted(true)` and `ownerRepository.save(owner)`, so the row is retained and the DELETE still returns 2xx (the pipeline's `RespondWithNoContent` \u2192 204). GET afterward still loads the row and returns it with `deleted: true`.\n\n**Create-endpoint duplicate/identity checks ignore deleted owners**\n- `function/owner/CheckOwnerIdentityUnique.java` \u2014 skips any existing owner flagged deleted, so a soft-deleted owner never triggers the 409 household-identity conflict.\n- `function/owner/AssignPossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 skips deleted owners in the soft-match scan.\n\n**API contract**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and MapStruct maps it automatically (name matches `Owner.getDeleted()`), giving the `$.deleted` JSON the tests assert on.\n\nThe two acceptance tests \u2014 soft-delete flags-and-retains, and deleted owner ignored by the duplicate check \u2014 both pass.",
